This is a research-only task — I did not modify any files. Here are the complete findings.

## 1. `gk_collisionless_flux.c` — constructor and struct populated

File: `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/zero/gk_collisionless_flux.c:13-74`

```c
gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux*
gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_new(const struct gkyl_rect_grid *phase_grid,
  const struct gkyl_basis *conf_basis, const struct gkyl_basis *phase_basis,
  const double charge, const double mass,
  enum gkyl_gk_collisionless_type type,
  const struct gk_geometry *gk_geom, const struct gkyl_dg_geom *dg_geom,
  const struct gkyl_gk_dg_geom *gk_dg_geom, const struct gkyl_velocity_map *vel_map,
  const enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type *bctype_conf, bool use_gpu)
```

Key points (lines 46-67):
- `bctype_conf` is a flat array of length `2*GKYL_MAX_CDIM`: `bctype_conf[d]` = lower-edge BC type for conf-dir `d`, `bctype_conf[GKYL_MAX_CDIM+d]` = upper-edge BC type for conf-dir `d`.
- Per direction `d` (0..cdim-1), the constructor calls the kernel choosers, passing the BC type only to select interior/edge kernels:
  - `up->flux_surf[d]` — chosen with a **hardcoded** `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_ABSORB` (comment: "BC option in ->flux_surf kernel doesn't matter as long as it's not SKIP") — line 49/60.
  - `up->flux_surf_edge_lo[d]` — chosen using `bctype_conf[d]` (real lower BC) — line 50-51/61-62.
  - `up->flux_surf_edge_up[d]` — chosen using `bctype_conf[GKYL_MAX_CDIM+d]` (real upper BC) — line 52-53/63-64.
- There is **no separate periodicity flag** passed in at all — only `bctype_conf` (an `enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type` per lower/upper edge per direction). The only distinction the chooser functions make is `bc == GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP` (→ pick "multib boundary" kernel table) vs. anything else, including `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC` (→ pick the regular/interior kernel table). So **periodicity itself is not directly tested inside this updater**; what matters is whether the BC type equals `GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP` or not. Non-`SKIP` covers both PERIODIC and all the various non-periodic species BCs (COPY, REFLECT, ABSORB, SHEATH, etc.) — they all route to the same (non-multib) kernel table.

## 2. Public header `gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux.h`

File: `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/zero/gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux.h:34-41`

```c
struct gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux* 
gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_new(const struct gkyl_rect_grid *phase_grid, 
  const struct gkyl_basis *conf_basis, const struct gkyl_basis *phase_basis, 
  const double charge, const double mass,
  enum gkyl_gk_collisionless_type collless_type,
  const struct gk_geometry *gk_geom, const struct gkyl_dg_geom *dg_geom, 
  const struct gkyl_gk_dg_geom *gk_dg_geom, const struct gkyl_velocity_map *vel_map,
  const enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type *bctype_conf, bool use_gpu);
```

Doxygen comment (line 30): `@param bctype_conf Type of conf-space BCs.`

### `enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type` — full definition

File: `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/zero/gkyl_gk_bc_type.h:8-29`

```c
enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type {
  // Particle BCs.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP = 0, // Do not apply any BCs 
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_COPY, // Copy skin into ghost.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_REFLECT, // Reflect particles.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_ABSORB, // Absorbing BCs.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_FUNC, // Fill ghost cell using a user-function.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_FIXED_FUNC, // Fixed function, time-independent.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_ZERO_FLUX, // Zero flux.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_SHEATH, // Sheath.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_RECYCLE, // Recycling.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC, // Periodic.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_TWISTSHIFT, // Twist-shift.
  GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_BOUNDARY_VALUE, // Skin value at the boundary.
  // Field BCs.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_PERIODIC, // Periodic.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_DIRICHLET, // Dirichlet.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_NEUMANN, // Nemann.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_DIRICHLET_VARYING, // Spatially varying Dirichlet.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_BOUNDARY_VALUE, // Skin value at the boundary.
  GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_TWISTSHIFT, // Twist-shift.
};
```

Note `GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP = 0` is explicitly documented as "Do not apply any BCs" — in the multiblock context, this is what a block face assigns to a direction/edge that is an *internal* multiblock boundary (i.e., not a true physical/periodic boundary of that block), signaling the collisionless-flux updater to use the "multib boundary" kernel variant instead of the ordinary boundary kernel.

Also relevant, `enum gkyl_gk_collisionless_type` (`/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/core/zero/gkyl_eqn_type.h:181-188`):
```c
enum gkyl_gk_collisionless_type {
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_NONE = 0,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_ES,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_ES_NO_BY,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_EM_BPERP,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_EM_BPAR,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_EM,
  GKYL_GK_COLLISIONLESS_NEUTRAL,
};
```

## 3. Call site — `gk_species_collisionless.c`

Only one call site in the repo: `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/apps/gk_species_collisionless.c:158-167`

```c
enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type bctype_conf[2*GKYL_MAX_CDIM];
for (int d=0; d<app->cdim; d++) {
  bctype_conf[d] = gks->lower_bc[d].type;
  bctype_conf[GKYL_MAX_CDIM+d] = gks->upper_bc[d].type;
}

gkcls->surf_flux_op = gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_new(&gks->grid, &app->basis, &gks->basis, 
  gks->info.charge, gks->info.mass,
  gkcls->collisionless_id, app->gk_geom, 
  app->dg_geom, app->gk_dg_geom, gks->vel_map, bctype_conf, app->use_gpu);
```

`gks->lower_bc[d].type` / `gks->upper_bc[d].type` are populated earlier in `gk_species.c:1470-1499` (`/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/apps/gk_species.c`):

```c
gks->num_periodic_dir = app->num_periodic_dir;
for (int d=0; d<gks->num_periodic_dir; ++d)
  gks->periodic_dirs[d] = app->periodic_dirs[d];

for (int d=0; d<app->cdim; ++d) gks->bc_is_np[d] = true;
for (int d=0; d<gks->num_periodic_dir; ++d)
  gks->bc_is_np[gks->periodic_dirs[d]] = false;

// Store the BCs from the input file.
for (int d=0; d<app->cdim; ++d) {
  if (gks->bc_is_np[d]) {
    struct gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc *bc_lo = gk_fetch_bc_with_dir_edge(gks->info.bcs, 2*app->cdim, d, GKYL_LOWER_EDGE);
    if (bc_lo != 0) gks->lower_bc[d] = *bc_lo;
    else            gks->lower_bc[d].type = GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP;
    struct gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc *bc_up = gk_fetch_bc_with_dir_edge(gks->info.bcs, 2*app->cdim, d, GKYL_UPPER_EDGE);
    if (bc_up != 0) gks->upper_bc[d] = *bc_up;
    else            gks->upper_bc[d].type = GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP;
  }
  else {
    gks->lower_bc[d].type = GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC;
    gks->upper_bc[d].type = GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC;
  }
}
```

**This is the key finding for your question**: `app->num_periodic_dir` / `app->periodic_dirs[]` (populated from the top-level app input, `gyrokinetic.c:191-193`, from `struct gkyl_gk` field `gkyl_gyrokinetic.h:619-620`, and similarly for multib apps `gyrokinetic_multib.c:101-103`/`gkyl_gyrokinetic_multib.h:164-165`) is exactly the app-level per-direction periodicity array that already exists at species-init time. It's turned into `gks->bc_is_np[d]` (bool, true = non-periodic) at `gk_species.c:1476-1478`, and then that boolean directly determines whether `gks->lower_bc[d].type`/`gks->upper_bc[d].type` get set to `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC` or to whatever real BC (or `GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP` if none given) was specified in the input file.

So: **at the time `gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_new` is called, only the resulting `enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type` per lower/upper/dir is passed in (`bctype_conf`) — not a raw periodicity boolean.** Periodicity information does exist earlier in the pipeline as `app->num_periodic_dir`/`app->periodic_dirs[]` (and `gks->bc_is_np[]`/`gks->periodic_dirs[]` copies), but by the time it reaches `gk_species_collisionless_init` (where the flux updater is constructed) it has already been collapsed into the `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC` enum value stored in `gks->lower_bc[d].type` / `gks->upper_bc[d].type`. If your planned changes to `gk_collisionless_flux.c`/`_priv.h` need to specifically distinguish PERIODIC from other non-SKIP BCs (rather than just SKIP vs. non-SKIP as currently done), you would either:
- test `bctype_conf[...] == GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC` directly inside the chooser functions (the enum value is already available, no new plumbing needed), or
- thread through `gks->bc_is_np[]`/`is_dir_periodic[]`-style bool array as an additional constructor parameter if you need it independent of the BC-type enum.

Note also `gks->bc_is_np[GKYL_MAX_CDIM]` and `gks->periodic_dirs[GKYL_MAX_CDIM]`/`num_periodic_dir` are stored fields on `struct gk_species` (defined at `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/apps/gkyl_gyrokinetic_priv.h:1060-1065`), so they are readily accessible at the `gk_species_collisionless_init` call site (`gkcls` init is called with `gks` in scope) without extra plumbing if you decide to pass a periodicity bool array instead of/alongside `bctype_conf`.

Analogous pattern also exists for neutral species (`gk_neut_species_kinetic.c:679-687`, `gk_neut_species_fluid.c:231-239`) and for the field itself (`gk_field_2x3x.c:530-531`, `gk_field_flr.c:39-40` compute a `bc_is_np[]` from `app->num_periodic_dir`/`app->periodic_dirs[]`), confirming this is the standard app-wide idiom for periodicity.

## 4. Private header `gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_priv.h`

File: `/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/zero/gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_priv.h`

### `struct gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux` (lines 41-58)

```c
struct gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux {
  struct gkyl_rect_grid phase_grid; // Phase space grid for cell spacing and cell center
  int cdim; // Configuration space dimensionality
  int pdim; // Phase space dimensionality
  gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t flux_surf[GKYL_MAX_CDIM]; // kernel for computing surface expansion of phase space flux.
  gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t flux_surf_edge_lo[GKYL_MAX_CDIM]; // kernel for computing surface expansion of phase space flux.
  gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t flux_surf_edge_up[GKYL_MAX_CDIM]; // kernel for computing surface expansion of phase space flux.
                                                        // at upper configuration space edge
  gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_t flux_surfvpar[1]; // kernel for computing surface expansion of phase space flux alpha
  double charge, mass;
  const struct gk_geometry *gk_geom; // Pointer to geometry struct.
  const struct gkyl_dg_geom *dg_geom; // Pointer to vol dg geometry struct.
  const struct gkyl_gk_dg_geom *gk_dg_geom; // Pointer to vol gk dg geometry struct.
  const struct gkyl_velocity_map *vel_map; // Velocity space mapping object.

  uint32_t flags;
  struct gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux *on_dev; // pointer to itself or device data
};
```

There is **no periodicity field stored** in this struct at all — only the resolved kernel function pointers (already baked in at construction time based on `bctype_conf`), plus grid/geometry/species data.

### Chooser function signatures (lines 310-426)

```c
// ES variant
static gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_surf_conf_kern(int dir, int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order, enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type bc);   // line 312

static gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_edge_surf_conf_kern(int dir, int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order, enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type bc);  // line 338

static gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_surf_vpar_kern(int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order);   // line 364 (no bc param)

// ES_NO_BY variant
static gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_no_by_surf_conf_kern(int dir, int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order, enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type bc);  // line 371

static gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_no_by_edge_surf_conf_kern(int dir, int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order, enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type bc);  // line 397

static gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_t
choose_gk_collisionless_flux_no_by_surf_vpar_kern(int cdim, int vdim, int poly_order);   // line 423 (no bc param)
```

Each of the `_surf_conf_kern`/`_edge_surf_conf_kern` choosers does exactly this (e.g. lines 312-334):

```c
if (bc == GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP) {
  // pick from the "multib_boundary" (or "no_by_multib_boundary") kernel table for this dir
} else {
  // pick from the regular (interior-style) kernel table for this dir
}
```

i.e., **`bc` is used solely as a boolean discriminator: `== GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP` vs. everything else** (there is no `switch` over all enum values — any non-`SKIP` value, including `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC`, `ABSORB`, `REFLECT`, `SHEATH`, etc., takes the same non-multib branch).

### Kernel list table struct definitions (lines 13-39)

```c
typedef double (*gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t)(const double *w, const double *dxv, 
  const double *vmap, const double *vmapSq, const double q_, const double m_, 
  const struct gkyl_dg_surf_geom *dgs, const struct gkyl_gk_dg_surf_geom *gkdgs, 
  const double *bmag, const double *jacobgeo_rat_surfL, const double *jacobgeo_rat_surfR,
  const double *phiL, const double *phiR, const double *JfL, const double *JfR,
  double* GKYL_RESTRICT yfieldL, double* GKYL_RESTRICT yfieldR, double* GKYL_RESTRICT flux_surf); 

typedef double (*gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_t)( 
  const double *w, const double *dxv, 
  const double *vmap_prime_l, const double *vmap_prime_r,
  const double *vmap, const double *vmapSq, const double q_, const double m_, 
  const struct gkyl_dg_vol_geom *dgv, const struct gkyl_gk_dg_vol_geom *gkdgv, 
  const double *bmag, const double *yfield, const double *JfL, const double *JfR, double* GKYL_RESTRICT flux_surf); 

// The cv_index[cd].vdim[vd] is used to index the various list of kernels below.
static struct { int vdim[3]; } cv_index[] = {
  {-1, -1, -1}, // 0x makes no sense.
  {-1,  0,  1}, // 1x kernel indices.
  {-1, -1,  2}, // 2x kernel indices.
  {-1, -1,  3}, // 3x kernel indices.
};

typedef struct { gk_collisionless_flux_surf_t kernels[3]; } gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_surf_kern_list;
typedef struct { gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_t kernels[3]; } gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_surfvpar_kern_list;
```

### Sample instantiation for indexing scheme (1x2v example, lines 65-70)

```c
static const gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_surf_kern_list ser_gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_kernels[] = {
  { NULL, gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_1x1v_ser_p1, NULL }, // 0
  { NULL, gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_1x2v_ser_p1, NULL }, // 1
  { NULL, gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_2x2v_ser_p1, NULL }, // 2
  { NULL, gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_3x2v_ser_p1, NULL }, // 3
};
```

Indexing scheme: outer array index = `cv_index[cdim].vdim[vdim]` (a combined config/velocity-dim code: 0 = 1x1v, 1 = 1x2v, 2 = 2x2v, 3 = 3x2v — per the `cv_index` table above, e.g. `cv_index[1].vdim[1] == 0` for 1x1v, `cv_index[1].vdim[2] == 1` for 1x2v, `cv_index[2].vdim[2] == 2` for 2x2v, `cv_index[3].vdim[2] == 3` for 3x2v). Inner array `.kernels[poly_order]` (fixed size 3: indices 0,1,2 for p=0,1,2, though currently only p1 kernels are populated — everything else is `NULL`). So for 1x2v at `poly_order=1`: `ser_gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_kernels[1].kernels[1] == gk_collisionless_flux_surfx_1x2v_ser_p1`.

There are 4 families of these tables (regular / edge / multib-boundary / multib-boundary-edge) × surfx/surfy/surfz × (ES with-by / ES no-by) — all following the identical `[cv_index].kernels[poly_order]` indexing.

### Summary answer to your core question

At construction time of `gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux`, the code does **not** carry an explicit `is_dir_periodic[cdim]` bool array or a raw `enum gkyl_edge` per boundary. It only receives `bctype_conf` (an `enum gkyl_gyrokinetic_bc_type` array of size `2*GKYL_MAX_CDIM`, lower dirs then upper dirs), and the only discrimination made internally is `bc == GKYL_BC_GK_SKIP` (used for multiblock "skip"/internal-boundary handling) vs. anything else (which includes periodic and all other physical non-periodic BCs, treated identically by this updater). The genuine periodicity information (`app->num_periodic_dir`, `app->periodic_dirs[]`, and species-level `gks->bc_is_np[]`/`gks->periodic_dirs[]`) is computed earlier in `gk_species.c` (`/Users/mfrancis/Documents/gkeyll/code/gkeyll_v0/gkeyll/gyrokinetic/apps/gk_species.c:1470-1499`) and is available in scope at the `gk_species_collisionless_init` call site if you want to thread it through as a new parameter, but currently it's collapsed into `GKYL_BC_GK_SPECIES_PERIODIC`/`GKYL_BC_GK_FIELD_PERIODIC` enum values before reaching `gkyl_gk_collisionless_flux_new`.
